          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe for treating an acute migraine, and when is prevention considered?",
          "answer": "**Acute treatment.** The migraine guidance (National headache society guidance, \"Migraine: acute treatment, when prevention starts, and medication-overuse limits\") says attacks are best treated early, while pain is still mild. It groups options by strength of evidence rather than ranking them for any individual:\n\n- Simple pain relievers and anti-inflammatory drugs for mild to moderate attacks.\n- Triptans for moderate to severe attacks, or when simple pain relievers have not worked.\n- Gepant and ditan drugs where triptans do not work, are not tolerated, or are unsafe because of heart or blood-vessel disease.\n- Anti-nausea medicines alongside treatment when nausea makes swallowing tablets unreliable.\n\nThe guidance also says a treatment should usually be tried on two or three separate attacks before it is judged ineffective.\n\n**When prevention is considered.** The same document says preventive treatment is offered when attacks are frequent or disabling enough that acute treatment alone is not enough. As a threshold, it cites four or more migraine days a month with some disability, or fewer attacks if they are severely disabling. Prevention is also considered when acute treatment does not work or cannot be used, or when the person prefers it. The aim is to reduce how often and how severely attacks happen, not to eliminate them. A 50 percent reduction in migraine days is the usual measure of a good response.\n\n**What this does not cover.** The search also returned guidance on anxiety, osteoarthritis, sinus and chest infections, flu, insomnia and blood pressure. None of it addresses migraine, so I have not used it. The migraine document does not give doses or name specific products, and I cannot offer those here. Choosing a treatment depends on your own history, so a doctor or pharmacist is the right person to discuss it with.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
